    "ym-build-representation-calculus",
    "Build and audit typed, phenomenon-preserving representation packages and morphisms for Yang--Mills and constructive QFT. Use when relating lattice, Euclidean, Hamiltonian, observable-algebraic, renormalization-group, probabilistic, or dual descriptions and when exact equivalence must be separated from approximation, coarse-graining, or conjectural correspondence.",
    "---\nname: ym-build-representation-calculus\ndescription: Build and audit typed, phenomenon-preserving representation packages and morphisms for Yang--Mills and constructive QFT. Use when relating lattice, Euclidean, Hamiltonian, observable-algebraic, renormalization-group, probabilistic, or dual descriptions and when exact equivalence must be separated from approximation, coarse-graining, or conjectural correspondence.\n---\n\n## Abducer Integration Guards\n\n- Activate this skill only for `yang-mills-existence-and-mass-gap-target` or an explicitly marked Yang-Mills calibration task.\n- Treat imported representational-dynamics artifacts as restricted, finite, conditional, or support evidence unless a later proof discharges their frontier hypotheses.\n- Emit claim level, non-claims, hard gate, hypotheses, falsifier, and demotion trigger before any promotion.\n- Compose with the target-neutral proof-depth skills: pressure formalization, hard-gate selection, nontriviality audit, anti-definitionality audit, same-domain transfer certification, and declarative learning extraction.\n- State efficient representation requirements: preserved observables, morphisms or recovery maps, invariants, error budgets, equivalence policy, locality or cutoff budget, transfer interface, and falsifiers.\n- Do not let a transfer theorem pass if the target property is built into the bridge definition; require independently stated source and target objects plus a consequence delta.\n- Preserve the four-cycle, recursion-depth-one publication-consolidation limit for local subgoals.\n- Source provenance: `agentic/external/yang_mills_representational_dynamics/2026-07-20/skill_bundle/11_vscode_codex_skill_bundle/.agents/skills/ym-build-representation-calculus/SKILL.md`.\n\n## Mathematical Invariants\n\nPreserve the Yang--Mills mathematical object: compact simple gauge group, pure gauge content, gauge-invariant observable algebra, regulator/cutoff/volume/coupling regime, reflection positivity, locality, OS reconstruction route, continuum limit order, and Hamiltonian spectral target. Any representation, morphism, RG step, or restricted model must state which invariants are preserved, which error budgets are allowed, and which edge case or counterexample would break transfer.\n\n## Epistemic Discipline\n\nEvery output must state epistemic status, claim level, evidence citation, source artifacts, explicit unknowns, and validation handoff. Treat physical relevance, literature-gap warrant, and frontier comparison as hard gates; unsupported implications stay conditional, blocked, or `requires_prior_art`.\n\n## Failure/Demotion\n\nDemote or quarantine if the skill assumes a mass gap, clustering, confinement, continuum existence, regulator independence, positivity, locality, observable density, or theory identity instead of proving it. Disconfirming evidence, prior-art collision, target-equivalent bridge definitions, or missing counterexample tests block promotion to a major YM publication pathway.\n\n# Build a Representation Calculus\n\n## Representation package\n\nUse\n`R = (K, A_phys, S, D, G, L, Q)`\nfor carrier, physical observables, states, dynamics/correlations, symmetries and redundancies, scale/limit structure, and quantitative controls. Type every component and parameter.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Define source and target packages independently of their presentation variables.\n2. Define observable pullback and state pushforward; state their compatibility equation.\n3. Label the map as exact equivalence, quotient, embedding, regularization, coarse-graining, asymptotic equivalence, reconstruction, continuum limit, duality, or heuristic correspondence.\n4. For approximate maps, give observable-indexed errors with cutoff, volume, scale, and support dependence.\n5. Attach certificates for positivity, locality, symmetry, theory identity, nontriviality, scaling, and spectral information.\n6. Prove identities, composition, and accumulated-error bounds.\n7. Test hostile examples: small observable classes, nonuniform limits, lost positivity, hidden sectors, and noncommuting limits.\n8. Emit a commutative-diagram obligation ledger and the strongest proved relation.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Never call numerical agreement or shared perturbation series an equivalence.\n- Do not treat an irreversible coarse-graining map as invertible.\n- Require recovery theorems for temporarily lost locality or covariance.\n- Keep physical time evolution distinct from gauge change, RG flow, and reconstruction.\n- Drop categorical language that does not sharpen a theorem or error bound.\n\n## Output contract\n\nReturn formal packages, typed morphisms, composition laws, certificate table, countermodels, exact uniformity domain, and unresolved analytic obligations.\n\n## Packaged PRISM Output Contract\n\nReturn exactly the generated `BoundedDomainFrontier` JSON object. Do not paraphrase closed variant values.\n\n- Follow the exact generated JSON schema without extra fields.\n",
    "{\"agents/openai.yaml\":\"\"}",
)
